- '@id':
    https://chris-page-gov.github.io/okf-dwp/id/legal-body/uksi/2002/1792/schedule/II/paragraph/4
  '@type': schema:CreativeWork
  route: legal-body/uksi/2002/1792/schedule/II/paragraph/4
  title: uksi/2002/1792/schedule/II/paragraph/4 — Circumstances in which a person is to be treated
    as occupying a dwelling as his home
  type: Statutory body evidence
  status: draft
  authority: Machine extraction from official legislation publication; unreviewed
  review_status: unreviewed
  resource: https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2002/1792/schedule/II/paragraph/4/2026-09-20
  license: https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/doc/open-government-licence/version/3/
  description: 'Requested source version 2026-09-20. Observed extent attributes: unknown. Complete selected
    unit with whitespace-normalised numbered blocks; editorial commentary and amendment structure remain
    in the linked projection. Legal applicability, commencement, complete effects and specialist review
    remain unestablished.'
  body: "4\n1\nSubject to the following provisions of this paragraph, a person shall be treated as occupying
    as his home the dwelling normally occupied as his home by himself or, if he has a partner, by himself
    and his partner, and he shall not be treated as occupying any other dwelling as his home.\n2\nIn determining
    whether a dwelling is the dwelling normally occupied as the claimant’s home for the purposes of sub-paragraph
    (1) regard shall be had to any other dwelling occupied by the claimant or by him and his partner whether
    or not that other dwelling is in Great Britain.\n3\nSubject to sub-paragraph (4), where a claimant
    who has no partner is a full-time student or is on a training course and is liable to make payments
    (including payments of mortgage interest or, in Scotland, payments under heritable securities or,
    in either case, analogous payments) in respect of either (but not both) the dwelling which he occupies
    for the purpose of attending his course of study or his training course or, as the case may be, the
    dwelling which he occupies when not attending his course, he shall be treated as occupying as his
    home the dwelling in respect of which he is liable to make payments.\n4\nA full-time student shall
    not be treated as occupying a dwelling as his home for any week of absence from it, other than an
    absence occasioned by the need to enter hospital for treatment, outside the period of study, if the
    main purpose of his occupation during the period of study would be to facilitate attendance on his
    course.\n5\nWhere a claimant has been required to move into temporary accommodation by reason of essential
    repairs being carried out to the dwelling normally occupied as his home and he is liable to make payments
    (including payments of mortgage interest or, in Scotland, payments under heritable securities or,
    in either case, analogous payments) in respect of either (but not both) the dwelling normally occupied
    or the temporary accommodation, he shall be treated as occupying as his home the dwelling in respect
    of which he is liable to make those payments.\n6\nWhere a person is liable to make payments in respect
    of two (but not more than two) dwellings, he shall be treated as occupying both dwellings as his home
    only—\na\nwhere he has left and remains absent from the former dwelling occupied as the home through
    fear of violence in that dwelling or of violence by a close relative or former partner and it is reasonable
    that housing costs should be met in respect of both his former dwelling and his present dwelling occupied
    as the home; or\nb\nin the case of partners, where one partner is a full-time student or is on a training
    course and it is unavoidable that he or they should occupy two separate dwellings and reasonable that
    housing costs should be met in respect of both dwellings; or\nc\nin the case where a person has moved
    into a new dwelling occupied as the home, except where sub-paragraph (5) applies, for a period not
    exceeding four benefit weeks from the first day of the benefit week where the move takes place on
    that day, but if it does not, from the first day of the next following benefit week if his liability
    to make payments in respect of two dwellings is unavoidable.\n7\nWhere—\na\na person has moved into
    a dwelling and was liable to make payments in respect of that dwelling before moving in; and\nb\n\
    he had claimed state pension credit before moving in and either that claim has not yet been determined
    or it has been determined but—\ni\nan amount has not been included under this Schedule; or\nii\nthe
    claim has been refused and a further claim has been made within four weeks of the date on which the
    claimant moved into the new dwelling occupied as the home; and\nc\nthe delay in moving into the dwelling
    in respect of which there was liability to make payments before moving in was reasonable and—\ni\n\
    that delay was necessary in order to adapt the dwelling to meet the disablement needs of the claimant,
    his partner or a person under the age of 20 for whom either the claimant or his partner is responsible;
    or\nii\nthe move was delayed pending local welfare provision or the outcome of an application under
    Part VIII of the 1992 Act for a social fund payment to meet a need arising out of the move or in connection
    with setting up the home in the dwelling; or\niii\nthe person became liable to make payments in respect
    of the dwelling while he was a patient or was in a care home or an independent hospital,\nhe shall
    be treated as occupying the dwelling as his home for any period not exceeding four weeks immediately
    prior to the date on which he moved into the dwelling and in respect of which he was liable to make
    payments.\n8\nThis sub-paragraph applies to a person who enters a care home or an independent hospital—\n\
    a\nfor the purpose of ascertaining whether that care home or independent hospital suits his needs,
    and\nb\nwith the intention of returning to the dwelling which he normally occupies as his home should,
    in the event that, the care home or independent hospital prove not to suit his needs,\nand while in
    the care home or independent hospital, the part of the dwelling which he normally occupies as his
    home is not let, or as the case may be, sub-let to another person.\n9\nA person to whom sub-paragraph
    (8) applies shall be treated as occupying the dwelling he normally occupies as his home during any
    period (commencing with the day he enters the care home or independent hospital) not exceeding 13
    weeks in which the person is resident in the care home or independent hospital, but only in so far
    as the total absence from the dwelling does not exceed 52 weeks.\n10\nA person, other than a person
    to whom sub-paragraph (1l) applies, shall be treated as occupying a dwelling as his home throughout
    any period of absence not exceeding 13 weeks, if, and only if—\na\nhe intends to return to occupy
    the dwelling as his home; and\nb\nthe part of the dwelling normally occupied by him has not been let
    or, as the case may be, sub-let to another person; and\nc\nthe period of absence is unlikely to exceed
    13 weeks.\n11\nThis sub-paragraph applies to a person whose absence from the dwelling he normally
    occupies as his home is temporary and—\na\nhe intends to return to occupy the dwelling as his home;
    and\nb\nwhile the part of the dwelling which is normally occupied by him has not been let or, as the
    case may be, sub-let; and\nc\nhe is—\ni\ndetained in custody on remand pending trial or, as a condition
    of bail, required to reside—\naa\nin a dwelling, other than the dwelling he occupies as his home;
    or\nbb\nin premises approved under section 13 of the Offender Management Act 2007 ,\nor, detained
    pending sentence upon conviction; or\nii\nresident in a hospital or similar institution as a patient;
    or\niii\nundergoing or, as the case may be, his partner or a person who has not attained the age of
    20 and who is dependent on him or his partner is undergoing, in the United Kingdom or elsewhere, medical
    treatment, or medically approved convalescence, in accommodation other than in a care home or an independent
    hospital; or\niv\nfollowing, in the United Kingdom or elsewhere, a training course; or\nv\nundertaking
    medically approved care of a person residing in the United Kingdom or elsewhere; or\nvi\nundertaking
    the care of a person under the age of 20 whose parent or guardian is temporarily absent from the dwelling
    normally occupied by that parent or guardian for the purpose of receiving medically approved care
    or medical treatment, or\nvii\na person who is, whether in the United Kingdom or elsewhere, receiving
    medically approved care provided in accommodation other than a care home or an independent hospital;
    or\nviii\na full-time student to whom sub-paragraph (3) or (6)(b) does not apply; or\nix\na person,
    other than a person to whom sub-paragraph (8) applies, who is receiving care provided in a care home
    or an independent hospital; or\nx\na person to whom sub-paragraph (6)(a) does not apply and who has
    left the dwelling he occupies as his home through fear of violence in that dwelling, or by a person
    who was formerly his partner or is a close relative; and\nd\nthe period of his absence is unlikely
    to exceed a period of 52 weeks or, in exceptional circumstances, is unlikely substantially to exceed
    that period.\n12\nA person to whom sub-paragraph (11) applies is to be treated as occupying the dwelling
    he normally occupies as his home during any period of absence not exceeding 52 weeks beginning with
    the first day of that absence.\n13\nIn this paragraph—\na\n“medically approved” means certified by
    a medical practitioner;\nb\n“training course” means such a course of training or instruction provided
    wholly or partly by or on behalf of or in pursuance of arrangements made with, or approved by or on
    behalf of, Skills Development Scotland, Scottish Enterprise, Highlands and Islands Enterprise, a government
    department or the Secretary of State."
  context_assembly:
    kind: evidence
    text: "4\n1\nSubject to the following provisions of this paragraph, a person shall be treated as occupying
      as his home the dwelling normally occupied as his home by himself or, if he has a partner, by himself
      and his partner, and he shall not be treated as occupying any other dwelling as his home.\n2\nIn
      determining whether a dwelling is the dwelling normally occupied as the claimant’s home for the
      purposes of sub-paragraph (1) regard shall be had to any other dwelling occupied by the claimant
      or by him and his partner whether or not that other dwelling is in Great Britain.\n3\nSubject to
      sub-paragraph (4), where a claimant who has no partner is a full-time student or is on a training
      course and is liable to make payments (including payments of mortgage interest or, in Scotland,
      payments under heritable securities or, in either case, analogous payments) in respect of either
      (but not both) the dwelling which he occupies for the purpose of attending his course of study or
      his training course or, as the case may be, the dwelling which he occupies when not attending his
      course, he shall be treated as occupying as his home the dwelling in respect of which he is liable
      to make payments.\n4\nA full-time student shall not be treated as occupying a dwelling as his home
      for any week of absence from it, other than an absence occasioned by the need to enter hospital
      for treatment, outside the period of study, if the main purpose of his occupation during the period
      of study would be to facilitate attendance on his course.\n5\nWhere a claimant has been required
      to move into temporary accommodation by reason of essential repairs being carried out to the dwelling
      normally occupied as his home and he is liable to make payments (including payments of mortgage
      interest or, in Scotland, payments under heritable securities or, in either case, analogous payments)
      in respect of either (but not both) the dwelling normally occupied or the temporary accommodation,
      he shall be treated as occupying as his home the dwelling in respect of which he is liable to make
      those payments.\n6\nWhere a person is liable to make payments in respect of two (but not more than
      two) dwellings, he shall be treated as occupying both dwellings as his home only—\na\nwhere he has
      left and remains absent from the former dwelling occupied as the home through fear of violence in
      that dwelling or of violence by a close relative or former partner and it is reasonable that housing
      costs should be met in respect of both his former dwelling and his present dwelling occupied as
      the home; or\nb\nin the case of partners, where one partner is a full-time student or is on a training
      course and it is unavoidable that he or they should occupy two separate dwellings and reasonable
      that housing costs should be met in respect of both dwellings; or\nc\nin the case where a person
      has moved into a new dwelling occupied as the home, except where sub-paragraph (5) applies, for
      a period not exceeding four benefit weeks from the first day of the benefit week where the move
      takes place on that day, but if it does not, from the first day of the next following benefit week
      if his liability to make payments in respect of two dwellings is unavoidable.\n7\nWhere—\na\na person
      has moved into a dwelling and was liable to make payments in respect of that dwelling before moving
      in; and\nb\nhe had claimed state pension credit before moving in and either that claim has not yet
      been determined or it has been determined but—\ni\nan amount has not been included under this Schedule;
      or\nii\nthe claim has been refused and a further claim has been made within four weeks of the date
      on which the claimant moved into the new dwelling occupied as the home; and\nc\nthe delay in moving
      into the dwelling in respect of which there was liability to make payments before moving in was
      reasonable and—\ni\nthat delay was necessary in order to adapt the dwelling to meet the disablement
      needs of the claimant, his partner or a person under the age of 20 for whom either the claimant
      or his partner is responsible; or\nii\nthe move was delayed pending local welfare provision or the
      outcome of an application under Part VIII of the 1992 Act for a social fund payment to meet a need
      arising out of the move or in connection with setting up the home in the dwelling; or\niii\nthe
      person became liable to make payments in respect of the dwelling while he was a patient or was in
      a care home or an independent hospital,\nhe shall be treated as occupying the dwelling as his home
      for any period not exceeding four weeks immediately prior to the date on which he moved into the
      dwelling and in respect of which he was liable to make payments.\n8\nThis sub-paragraph applies
      to a person who enters a care home or an independent hospital—\na\nfor the purpose of ascertaining
      whether that care home or independent hospital suits his needs, and\nb\nwith the intention of returning
      to the dwelling which he normally occupies as his home should, in the event that, the care home
      or independent hospital prove not to suit his needs,\nand while in the care home or independent
      hospital, the part of the dwelling which he normally occupies as his home is not let, or as the
      case may be, sub-let to another person.\n9\nA person to whom sub-paragraph (8) applies shall be
      treated as occupying the dwelling he normally occupies as his home during any period (commencing
      with the day he enters the care home or independent hospital) not exceeding 13 weeks in which the
      person is resident in the care home or independent hospital, but only in so far as the total absence
      from the dwelling does not exceed 52 weeks.\n10\nA person, other than a person to whom sub-paragraph
      (1l) applies, shall be treated as occupying a dwelling as his home throughout any period of absence
      not exceeding 13 weeks, if, and only if—\na\nhe intends to return to occupy the dwelling as his
      home; and\nb\nthe part of the dwelling normally occupied by him has not been let or, as the case
      may be, sub-let to another person; and\nc\nthe period of absence is unlikely to exceed 13 weeks.\n\
      11\nThis sub-paragraph applies to a person whose absence from the dwelling he normally occupies
      as his home is temporary and—\na\nhe intends to return to occupy the dwelling as his home; and\n\
      b\nwhile the part of the dwelling which is normally occupied by him has not been let or, as the
      case may be, sub-let; and\nc\nhe is—\ni\ndetained in custody on remand pending trial or, as a condition
      of bail, required to reside—\naa\nin a dwelling, other than the dwelling he occupies as his home;
      or\nbb\nin premises approved under section 13 of the Offender Management Act 2007 ,\nor, detained
      pending sentence upon conviction; or\nii\nresident in a hospital or similar institution as a patient;
      or\niii\nundergoing or, as the case may be, his partner or a person who has not attained the age
      of 20 and who is dependent on him or his partner is undergoing, in the United Kingdom or elsewhere,
      medical treatment, or medically approved convalescence, in accommodation other than in a care home
      or an independent hospital; or\niv\nfollowing, in the United Kingdom or elsewhere, a training course;
      or\nv\nundertaking medically approved care of a person residing in the United Kingdom or elsewhere;
      or\nvi\nundertaking the care of a person under the age of 20 whose parent or guardian is temporarily
      absent from the dwelling normally occupied by that parent or guardian for the purpose of receiving
      medically approved care or medical treatment, or\nvii\na person who is, whether in the United Kingdom
      or elsewhere, receiving medically approved care provided in accommodation other than a care home
      or an independent hospital; or\nviii\na full-time student to whom sub-paragraph (3) or (6)(b) does
      not apply; or\nix\na person, other than a person to whom sub-paragraph (8) applies, who is receiving
      care provided in a care home or an independent hospital; or\nx\na person to whom sub-paragraph (6)(a)
      does not apply and who has left the dwelling he occupies as his home through fear of violence in
      that dwelling, or by a person who was formerly his partner or is a close relative; and\nd\nthe period
      of his absence is unlikely to exceed a period of 52 weeks or, in exceptional circumstances, is unlikely
      substantially to exceed that period.\n12\nA person to whom sub-paragraph (11) applies is to be treated
      as occupying the dwelling he normally occupies as his home during any period of absence not exceeding
      52 weeks beginning with the first day of that absence.\n13\nIn this paragraph—\na\n“medically approved”
      means certified by a medical practitioner;\nb\n“training course” means such a course of training
      or instruction provided wholly or partly by or on behalf of or in pursuance of arrangements made
      with, or approved by or on behalf of, Skills Development Scotland, Scottish Enterprise, Highlands
      and Islands Enterprise, a government department or the Secretary of State."
