- '@id': https://chris-page-gov.github.io/okf-dwp/id/legal-body/uksi/2013/376/regulation/3
  '@type': schema:CreativeWork
  route: legal-body/uksi/2013/376/regulation/3
  title: uksi/2013/376/regulation/3 — INTRODUCTION / The Benefit Unit / Couples
  type: Statutory body evidence
  status: draft
  authority: Machine extraction from official legislation publication; unreviewed
  review_status: unreviewed
  resource: https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2013/376/regulation/3/2026-09-20
  license: https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/doc/open-government-licence/version/3/
  description: 'Requested source version 2026-09-20. Observed extent attributes: E+W+S. Complete selected
    unit with whitespace-normalised numbered blocks; editorial commentary and amendment structure remain
    in the linked projection. Legal applicability, commencement, complete effects and specialist review
    remain unestablished.'
  body: "3\n1\nThis regulation makes provision in relation to couples, including cases where both members
    of a couple may be entitled to universal credit jointly without each of them meeting all the basic
    conditions referred to in section 4 of the Act (see paragraph (2)) and cases where a person whose
    partner does not meet all the basic conditions or is otherwise excluded from entitlement to universal
    credit may make a claim as a single person (see paragraph (3)).\n2\nA couple may be entitled to universal
    credit as joint claimants where—\na\none member does not meet the basic condition in section 4(1)(b)
    (under the qualifying age for state pension credit) if the other member does meet that condition;
    or\nb\none member does not meet the basic condition in section 4(1)(d) (not receiving education) and
    is not excepted from that condition if the other member does meet that condition or is excepted from
    it.\n3\nA person who is a member of a couple may make a claim as a single person if the other member
    of the couple—\na\ndoes not meet the basic condition in section 4(1)(a) (at least 18 years old) and
    is not a person in respect of whom the minimum age specified in regulation 8 applies;\nb\ndoes not
    meet the basic condition in section 4(1)(c) (in Great Britain);\nc\nis a prisoner;. . .\nd\nis a person
    other than a prisoner in respect of whom entitlement does not arise by virtue of regulation 19 (restrictions
    on entitlement) ; or\ne\nis a person to whom section 115 of the Immigration and Asylum Act 1999 (exclusion
    from benefits) applies,\nand regulations 18 (capital limit), 36 (amount of elements) and 22 (deduction
    of income and work allowance) provide for the calculation of the award in such cases.\n4\nWhere two
    people are parties to a polygamous marriage, the fact that they are husband and wife is to be disregarded
    if—\na\none of them is a party to an earlier marriage that still subsists; and\nb\nthe other party
    to that earlier marriage is living in the same household,\nand, accordingly, the person who is not
    a party to the earlier marriage may make a claim for universal credit as a single person.\n5\nIn paragraph
    (4) “polygamous marriage” means a marriage during which a party to it is married to more than one
    person and which took place under the laws of a country which permits polygamy.\n6\nWhere the claimant
    is a member of a couple, and the other member is temporarily absent from the claimant's household,
    they cease to be treated as a couple if that absence is expected to exceed, or does exceed, 6 months."
  context_assembly:
    kind: evidence
    text: "3\n1\nThis regulation makes provision in relation to couples, including cases where both members
      of a couple may be entitled to universal credit jointly without each of them meeting all the basic
      conditions referred to in section 4 of the Act (see paragraph (2)) and cases where a person whose
      partner does not meet all the basic conditions or is otherwise excluded from entitlement to universal
      credit may make a claim as a single person (see paragraph (3)).\n2\nA couple may be entitled to
      universal credit as joint claimants where—\na\none member does not meet the basic condition in section
      4(1)(b) (under the qualifying age for state pension credit) if the other member does meet that condition;
      or\nb\none member does not meet the basic condition in section 4(1)(d) (not receiving education)
      and is not excepted from that condition if the other member does meet that condition or is excepted
      from it.\n3\nA person who is a member of a couple may make a claim as a single person if the other
      member of the couple—\na\ndoes not meet the basic condition in section 4(1)(a) (at least 18 years
      old) and is not a person in respect of whom the minimum age specified in regulation 8 applies;\n\
      b\ndoes not meet the basic condition in section 4(1)(c) (in Great Britain);\nc\nis a prisoner;.
      . .\nd\nis a person other than a prisoner in respect of whom entitlement does not arise by virtue
      of regulation 19 (restrictions on entitlement) ; or\ne\nis a person to whom section 115 of the Immigration
      and Asylum Act 1999 (exclusion from benefits) applies,\nand regulations 18 (capital limit), 36 (amount
      of elements) and 22 (deduction of income and work allowance) provide for the calculation of the
      award in such cases.\n4\nWhere two people are parties to a polygamous marriage, the fact that they
      are husband and wife is to be disregarded if—\na\none of them is a party to an earlier marriage
      that still subsists; and\nb\nthe other party to that earlier marriage is living in the same household,\n\
      and, accordingly, the person who is not a party to the earlier marriage may make a claim for universal
      credit as a single person.\n5\nIn paragraph (4) “polygamous marriage” means a marriage during which
      a party to it is married to more than one person and which took place under the laws of a country
      which permits polygamy.\n6\nWhere the claimant is a member of a couple, and the other member is
      temporarily absent from the claimant's household, they cease to be treated as a couple if that absence
      is expected to exceed, or does exceed, 6 months."
